//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** \page relocation_docu Vertex Relocation during Decimation

  The classic decimation modules perform a halfedge collapse that moves the
  removed vertex onto the retained endpoint.  On curved surfaces the
  optimal position for the merged vertex often lies somewhere between (or
  beyond) the two endpoints, so an endpoint-only collapse cannot reach it.

  \section relocation_enable Enabling relocation

  OpenMesh::Decimater::BaseDecimaterT::set_relocation(bool) switches this
  behaviour on or off.  The default is off, which preserves the original
  calls, ordering and results exactly.  Changing the flag resets the
  decimater; call initialize() again afterwards.

  \code
  decimater.set_relocation(true);
  if (!decimater.initialize()) { ... }  // unsupported combination
  \endcode

  \section relocation_supported Supported configurations

  Relocation is accepted only when

  - the priority module is non-binary and reports
    ModBaseT::supports_relocation() (true for
    OpenMesh::Decimater::ModQuadricT), and
  - every additional module reports
    ModBaseT::relocation_compatible() (true for
    OpenMesh::Decimater::ModNormalFlippingT and
    OpenMesh::Decimater::ModAspectRatioT).

  A binary Quadric module, an arbitrary binary module, or a custom priority
  module without relocation support makes initialize() return false and
  leaves the mesh untouched.

  \section relocation_placement Target placement

  For each candidate the priority module supplies a target position through
  ModBaseT::relocate().  ModQuadricT places the retained vertex at the
  unconstrained minimizer of the merged quadric when the interior edge's
  two endpoints are not on the boundary, are unlocked and carry no feature
  flag.  A 3x3 system is treated as numerically singular when a pivot falls
  below 1e-12 times the largest absolute matrix entry (a zero matrix is
  singular as well); a singular or non-finite solution falls back to the
  lowest-error of p1, p0 and their midpoint, preferring p1, then p0, then
  the midpoint on ties.  All other candidates stay at p1.  The max_err
  bound is evaluated at the actual target position.

  \section relocation_consistency Consistency of score and collapse

  The target used to score a heap entry is stored with that entry and is
  reused, together with the binary modules, when the entry is popped.  If
  relocation changed the retained coordinate, the collapse applies the new
  point before refreshing face normals and module caches, so later
  candidates see the current geometry and the merged quadric.  A trial
  evaluation never leaves coordinates, normals or cached values modified.

  \section relocation_example Example

  \include relocation.cc

*/
